WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.210
A nuclear-armed North Korea, increasing aggression
from China, and space, cyber, and electronic

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.210
warfare are growing threats Japan faces in
a contested region.

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.480
Will Japan be able to maintain a solely defensive
military approach?

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:53.930
Guided by a seventy-year pacifist constitution,
Japan’s Self-Defense Forces (SDF) have never

00:00:53.930 --> 00:01:00.080
been ordered into combat.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:15.450
But for decades they have helped to maintain
global security.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:19.550
Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s cabinet reinterpreted
the constitution in 2014, allowing the SDF

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:20.550
to work with other militaries, including the
United States’.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:22.440
In a 1960 security treaty, the U.S. pledged
to defend Japan in the event of an attack.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:23.460
But U.S. overseas military commitments are
under increasing scrutiny in Washington.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:24.659
And an ever-present challenge for Japan is
China’s growing military dominance in Asia.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:25.869
Now, some supporters of constitutional change
think a stronger Japanese military is needed

00:01:25.869 --> 00:01:27.250
to defend the country and would be an asset
to the U.S. in maritime disputes.

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:28.250
But the issue remains contentious.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.250
Some Japanese citizens worry that the nation
will lose its pacifist identity.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:30.471
One thing is clear: how Japan chooses to use
its military in the future could change the

00:01:30.471 --> 00:01:31.471
balance of power in Asia.

00:01:31.471 --> 00:01:36.470
Learn more about how Japan is reassessing
its military power.

00:01:36.470 --> 00:01:40.229
Read Japan Rearmed by Sheila A. Smith.

